    <h1>Forest Raven Range: Where This Species Lives</h1>
  </header>

  <section class="qa-snippet" aria-label="Direct answer">
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> The Forest Raven (<em>Corvus tasmanicus</em>) is the dominant corvid on Tasmania (present island-wide) and occurs in disjunct populations on the mainland — coastal Victoria (Otways, Wilsons Promontory), Kangaroo Island and southern South Australia, and south-eastern New South Wales coastal ranges. On the mainland it is restricted to tall wet forest and is far less widespread than the Australian Raven.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Distribution</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> The Forest Raven's range is centred on Tasmania with small mainland outposts:<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Tasmania:</strong> present throughout the island in all major habitat types — tall wet forest (the core habitat), dry sclerophyll woodland, agricultural areas, and even urban Hobart. The only native corvid on Tasmania; the Australian Raven and Little Raven do not reach the island. Population robust and widespread.</li>
      <li><strong>Victoria (mainland):</strong> coastal Otway Ranges, Wilsons Promontory, and scattered localities in tall wet forest from about Lorne to the South Gippsland ranges. Small, sedentary populations.</li>
      <li><strong>South Australia:</strong> Kangaroo Island (resident) and southern Mount Lofty Ranges south-east of Adelaide (small population).</li>
      <li><strong>New South Wales:</strong> far south-east coastal ranges, potentially extending into Nadgee and Mimosa Rocks areas.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>The mainland populations are geographically isolated from one another and from the Tasmanian population. They are likely relicts of a formerly more continuous distribution along tall wet forest.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Habitat</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> The Forest Raven is more habitat-specialist than the Australian Raven on the mainland:<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Primary mainland habitat:</strong> tall wet eucalyptus forest (mountain ash, messmate, blue gum associations). Requires tall trees for nesting; strongly associated with intact or near-intact tall forest.</li>
      <li><strong>Tasmania:</strong> more flexible — uses a wide range of habitats including cleared farmland, urban areas, and alpine moorland, in addition to tall forest. This broader habitat use on Tasmania (without competition from other Australian corvids) explains why it is so widespread there.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

